right_speech:
Abstention from false speech, from divisive speech, from harsh speech, and from idle chatter. The four items each have their own criterion, and are not ranked by gravity:

- **False speech** — speaking contrary to fact: having seen, to say one has not seen; having heard, to say one has not heard (*Yogācārabhūmi-śāstra*, fascicle 8, has "having seen, heard, cognised, or known, to say that one has not seen, heard, cognised, or known");
- **Divisive speech** — speaking words that break up those who are in harmony, and words that keep those already divided from coming together; the criterion lies in **whether one wishes to damage the relations of others**, so that speech which is untrue may at the same time also be false speech, while speech which is true may still be divisive speech;
- **Harsh speech** — words that are coarse and grating and that provoke anger in others; the criterion lies in **the coarseness of the speech and the person addressed**;
- **Idle chatter** — untimely speech, meaningless speech, speech contrary to the Dharma; the criterion lies in **whether it has meaning and whether it is timely**, not in whether the topic is serious.

The treatises therefore define "abstention from idle chatter" by its opposite, and the *Yogācārabhūmi-śāstra*, Bodhisattva-bhūmi, lists the "seven kinds of speech": **timely speech, true speech, meaningful speech, Dharma speech, speech in accord with reason, speech that skillfully tames, and speech decided after weighing the occasion** — abstention from idle chatter does not mean that one may not speak, but that whatever is spoken is timely, true, meaningful, Dharma, reasonable, capable of taming, and well weighed.

Right speech belongs to the training in ethics within the [[eightfold|noble eightfold path]]. The four items each address something different: false speech addresses **facts**, divisive speech addresses **the relations between others**, harsh speech addresses **the other person**, and idle chatter addresses **the meaning of the speech itself**.

five_precepts:
Not killing, not stealing, not committing sexual misconduct, not lying, not drinking intoxicants. They are the foundation of the [[sila|training in ethics]] and a simplified form of the ten wholesome courses of action. Each of the five has its own criterion and is not merely a prohibition:

- **Not killing** — killing = **cutting off the life of a sentient being**. For the offence to be complete the conditions must all be present: it is a sentient being, one takes it as a sentient being, one gives rise to the thought of killing, one applies the means, and the life of the being in question is cut off (*Abhidharmakośa-bhāṣya*, fascicle 14, "Exposition of Action," on the characteristics of the ten courses of action);
- **Not stealing** — an object owned by another, one takes it as owned, one gives rise to the thought of stealing, one takes it away from its original place, and **the value amounting to five māṣas** makes it a grave offence (*Dharmaguptaka Vinaya*, Pārājika, the precept on stealing);
- **Not committing sexual misconduct** — **not a proper partner, not a proper orifice, not a proper time, not a proper place**: to do so with one who is not a spouse, by a non-vaginal orifice, at an unsuitable time, or in an unsuitable place;
- **Not lying** — **speaking contrary to fact**, having seen to say one has not seen, having heard to say one has not heard;
- **Not drinking intoxicants** — to drink **any liquor that can intoxicate**, whether grain liquor, fruit liquor, or medicinal liquor.

**The distinction between precepts by nature and precepts of prohibition** (the criterion lying in "whether they are evil in themselves," not in the object they are directed towards): the first four belong to the **precepts by nature** — evil in themselves, not waiting for a rule laid down by the Buddha; whether or not the Buddha has laid down the rule, to transgress them is always an offence. Not drinking intoxicants belongs to the **precepts of prohibition** — not evil in themselves, but because it is the root of heedlessness and can lead people to break the first four, the Buddha laid it down in order to protect the remaining precepts and to put an end to reproach. The old saying that "the first four arise with respect to the object of a sentient being and drinking arises with respect to a non-sentient object" is only a **concomitant feature**, not the criterion of classification.

Further, the first three precepts guard the body, the fourth guards speech, and the fifth guards both body and speech and so protects the first four.

There are many alternative names, and from them their meaning can be glimpsed: the [[doc_33|Abhidharma-mahāvibhāṣā]] calls them the "five fields of training," the *Sūtrālaṃkāra* (Da Zhuangyan Jing) calls them the "five great gifts," and the *Abhidharmakośa-bhāṣya* calls them the "lay vows" (the lay follower being the upāsaka).

**The dispute over partial and complete reception**: the *Ekottarika-āgama*, the *Upāsaka-śīla Sūtra* (Youposai Jie Jing), and the *Mahāparinirvāṇa Sūtra* put forward the view that they may be undertaken separately; the *Sarvāstivāda-vinaya-vibhāṣā* and the *Saṃyuktābhidharma-hṛdaya-śāstra* hold that the talk of "few-factor practice, many-factor practice, complete-factor practice" merely displays how much merit the observance of the precepts yields and does not mean that there is a method of receiving them separately, so that partial reception is not allowed.

four_immeasurables:
Loving-kindness, compassion, sympathetic joy, and equanimity. They are an important method for cultivating concentration and the basis of the Mahāyāna aspiration to awakening. They are also called the "four divine abidings" (brahmavihāra), because their fruit can be rebirth in the Brahma heavens.

- Loving-kindness (mettā / maitrī): the wish that beings obtain happiness;
- Compassion (karuṇā): the wish that beings be free from suffering;
- Sympathetic joy (muditā): rejoicing when beings obtain happiness, counteracting envy;
- Equanimity (upekṣā): **being without partiality towards beings**, free from liking and aversion. "Without discrimination" here means "without partisanship" and **not "making no distinctions"** — according to the *Visuddhimagga*, fascicle 9, "The Divine Abidings," and the *Abhidharmakośa-bhāṣya*, fascicle 29, equanimity, through the contemplation that "karma is one's own," becomes free from greed and hatred while still knowing beings and their suffering and happiness as they really are; it merely does not give rise to liking and aversion on that account.

The sense of "immeasurable" (apramāṇa) lies in the immeasurability of the object: the attitude is not directed at one particular being but extends to every direction and every being, and so the measure of the mind is unbounded. The order of the four is not accidental: loving-kindness and compassion have a definite orientation (giving happiness, removing suffering); "sympathetic joy" further counteracts displeasure at seeing others obtain happiness; and "equanimity" prevents the first three from falling into partiality — without equanimity, loving-kindness may become favouritism and compassion may become indulgence.

Their place in the cultivation of concentration: the four immeasurable attitudes can be developed up to the level of the "third dhyāna" (sympathetic joy) and the "fourth dhyāna" (equanimity), and are one of the specialised objects of calm (śamatha) practice; in the Mahāyāna they are the preliminary to the aspiration to awakening — one first softens the character with the four immeasurable attitudes, and then generates the aspiration, "for the benefit of all beings, may I attain Buddhahood."

four_dhyanas:
The four dhyānas of the [[three_realms|form realm]] and the four concentrations of the formless realm, together called the four dhyānas and eight concentrations. These are the levels of concentration described under [[right_samadhi|right concentration]].

The four dhyānas of the form realm:
- First dhyāna: secluded from sense-desires and unwholesome dharmas, with applied and sustained attention, with joy and happiness born of seclusion;
- Second dhyāna: applied and sustained attention stilled, inner clarity, with joy and happiness born of concentration;
- Third dhyāna: free of joy, dwelling in equanimity, with mindfulness and clear comprehension, experiencing happiness with the body ("the marvellous happiness of freedom from joy");
- Fourth dhyāna: equanimity and purity of mindfulness, neither-painful-nor-pleasant feeling.

The four concentrations of the formless realm are entered successively by way of different objects (*Abhidharmakośa-bhāṣya*, fascicle 28); the four are both concentrations and the places of rebirth of the four formless heavens:

- **The sphere of infinite space** — disenchanted with the perception of form, taking **infinite space** as object;
- **The sphere of infinite consciousness** — disenchanted with the infinity of space, taking **infinite consciousness** as object;
- **The sphere of nothingness** — disenchanted with the infinity of consciousness, taking **nothingness** as object;
- **The sphere of neither-perception-nor-non-perception** — **perception that is neither existent nor non-existent**, subtle and hard to discern, the summit of the three realms; its lifespan is eighty thousand great aeons, and when that karmic fruit is exhausted one still falls, so that it is not liberation (see the *Visuddhimagga*, fascicle 10, "The Formless Spheres").

The śāstras describe the four dhyānas with the "factors" (dhyāna-aṅga) as their framework: the first dhyāna has five factors (applied attention, sustained attention, joy, happiness, one-pointedness of mind); the second has four (inner clarity, joy, happiness, one-pointedness of mind); the third has five (equanimity, mindfulness, clear comprehension, happiness, one-pointedness of mind); the fourth has four (equanimity, purity of mindfulness, neither-painful-nor-pleasant feeling, one-pointedness of mind).

Two points call for attention: first, the four dhyānas and eight concentrations are not unique to Buddhism, and non-Buddhists can attain them too, so that they are a "common practice"; second, the fourth dhyāna is the support for Buddhist insight and for the development of supernormal powers — the Buddha's awakening and his entry into nirvāṇa both occurred in the fourth dhyāna, because in that dhyāna equanimity and purity of mindfulness are present and the mind is most serviceable.

thirty_seven_bodhipakkhiya:
The thirty-seven items of practice leading to awakening, the most complete unfolding of the "[[marga|truth of the path]]" in early Buddhism. They fall into seven groups:

1. **[[four_foundations|The four establishments of mindfulness]]** — contemplation of body, feeling, mind, and dharmas;
2. **The four right efforts** — to cause arisen evil to be cut off, to prevent unarisen evil from arising, to cause unarisen good to arise, to cause arisen good to increase;
3. **The four bases of success** (also called the four bases of supernormal power) — **desire** (wishing to attain concentration), **effort** (exertion and incitement), **mind** (one-pointed attention), and **investigation** (discernment by wisdom); all four are the supports for "accomplishing concentration," hence the name bases of success;
4. **The five faculties** — **faith** (pure confidence in the three jewels and the four noble truths), **effort** (diligence), **mindfulness** (clear retention without forgetting), **concentration** (one-pointedness of mind), and **wisdom** (discernment of dharmas) — because these five can produce wholesome dharmas they are called faculties;
5. **The five powers** — the same five grown to the point of being unmoved by defilements;
6. **The seven factors of enlightenment** — **mindfulness** (clearly remembering the object), **investigation of dharmas** (discerning the true from the false by wisdom), **effort**, **joy** (joy arising from attaining the dharma), **tranquillity** (body and mind attuned and at ease), **concentration**, and **equanimity** (the mind equal and impartial); these are the factors of enlightenment that arise in sequence at the stage of seeing the path;
7. **The noble eightfold path** — see [[eightfold|the noble eightfold path]] (right view, right thought, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, right concentration); the criterion of each factor is given under its own node.

These seven groups are not seven parallel methods but **different cross-sections of one and the same process of practice**: the four establishments of mindfulness are the point of entry, the four right efforts the incitement, the four bases of success the power of concentration, the five faculties and five powers the growth and firming of mental strength, the seven factors of enlightenment the sequence of awakening-awareness as one approaches the seeing of the path, and the noble eightfold path the general outline of the whole. The śāstras therefore regard them as the inventory of the content of the "truth of the path."

Among them the position of the "seven factors of enlightenment" is the most telling: it describes the **sequence of mental activity before and after the seeing of the path** (mindfulness → investigation of dharmas → effort → joy → tranquillity → concentration → equanimity), the turning point of practice from exertion to letting go, and it is therefore often singled out as a checklist for meditation.

sravaka_fruits:
The four fruitions of the hearer's practice, each having a "path" (the stage of practice tending towards that fruit) and a "fruit" (the stage already realised), together forming the "four paths and four fruits":

- **Sotāpanna** (stream-entry) — cuts off the three fetters (personality view, clinging to rules and vows, doubt), and at most returns seven times among gods and humans before attaining liberation;
- **Sakadāgāmin** (once-returner) — the three fetters are cut off and greed, hatred, and delusion are attenuated; returns once more to the [[three_realms|realm of desire]];
- **Anāgāmin** (non-returner) — cuts off the **five lower fetters** (personality view, clinging to rules and vows, doubt, sense-desire, hatred), is no longer reborn in the realm of desire, and attains [[nirvana|nirvāṇa]] in the form realm;
- **Arhat** (worthy of offerings, without birth) — cuts off the five higher fetters (greed for the form realm, greed for the formless realm, conceit, restlessness, ignorance): "birth is exhausted, the holy life has been lived, what had to be done has been done, there is no more coming to any state of being."

**Two structural points deserve attention**

1. **The dividing line between the path of seeing and the path of cultivation** — the first fruit is the "path of seeing" (seeing the truth of the four noble truths and cutting off the defilements of view at once), and the last three fruits are the "path of cultivation" (gradually cutting off the defilements of cultivation). This division was retained by all the schools and by the later Mahāyāna theories of the stages: **the question of the relation between sudden awakening and gradual cultivation was first raised within the framework of the four fruits**.
2. **The sequence of fetters cut off has content** — it is not a set of abstract stage names but a concrete inventory of the defilements cut off (the three fetters, the five lower fetters, the five higher fetters). The four fruits are therefore "verifiable milestones of practice," not purely a hierarchy of belief.

The Mahāyāna has two ways of assessing them: the Consciousness-Only school takes the four fruits as the ultimate stage of the "Hīnayāna" and as genuinely existing persons; Tiantai and Huayan, on the other hand, take them as a phantom city within the Mahāyāna path (the "parable of the phantom city" in the *Lotus Sūtra*), a resting place established as an expedient means.

vinaya_rites:
The actual operation of [[precepts|the precepts]] within the monastic community — the third level of the "[[sila|training in ethics]]": the precepts themselves (their marks), the essence of the precepts (obtained at ordination), and **monastic affairs (how to live and decide together)**.

- **Uposatha** — assembly every half month (on the fourteenth or fifteenth day) to recite the Prātimokṣa (the precepts), each monk confessing his own offences. This is the community's regular mechanism of self-purification, and the means by which the precepts are remembered and enforced in common;
- **The rains retreat** (varṣā) — during the three months of the rainy season (in China, from the fifteenth of the fourth month to the fifteenth of the seventh month) one does not wander, but dwells in one place for practice, avoiding the trampling of plants, insects, and ants and avoiding breaches of decorum through travelling; at the end of the retreat there is the **Pravāraṇā**, at which the monks are invited to point out one's own faults;
- **Karma** — the procedure by which the community deliberates and decides, in three forms: the **single motion** (one announcement and the matter is settled, used for routine affairs), the **motion with one resolution** (one announcement and one karma, used for middling affairs such as the reception of precepts), and the **motion with three resolutions** (one announcement and three karmas, used for the weightiest affairs, such as the conferring of full ordination, punishment, and the settlement of disputes), used for ordination, confession, the assignment of lodgings, the settlement of disputes, and every other monastic affair. **Every karma requires a "pure community" and a quorum; if the procedure is not in order, the matter does not stand.**

The significance of this level is that it shows the Buddhist precepts to be not a list of individual ethics but a set of **communal institutions** — enforceable, traceable, and repairable. The Vinaya collections of the Theravāda and the Sarvāstivāda preserve the most complete records of the procedures of these three observances; the *Dharmaguptaka Vinaya*, on which the Chinese Vinaya school relies, belongs to the Dharmaguptaka system.

link_clinging:
The four kinds of clinging — clinging to sense desire, clinging to views, clinging to rules and vows, and clinging to the doctrine of self — are the intensification and hardening of "craving."

- Clinging to sense desire: grasping at the five strands of sense desire, the hardening of craving at the sensory level;
- Clinging to views: grasping at various opinions and theories, holding that "only this is true and all else is false";
- Clinging to rules and vows: taking useless austerities, precepts, or taboos as the cause of liberation, such as clinging to the cow vow or the dog vow;
- Clinging to the doctrine of self: grasping at the various statements concerning "self," the most fundamental of the four clingings. **Its boundary with clinging to views**: clinging to views grasps "a certain view as supreme," its object being views; clinging to the doctrine of self grasps "there is a self," its object being the positing of "self" — the former, though it may also involve the view of self, comes down to "whose doctrine is supreme," while the latter comes down to "what the self is."

The classificatory significance of the four clingings is that they extend the clinging of [[samsara|saṃsāra]] from desire to theory and self-identification — erudition and austerity can equally become the bonds of saṃsāra.
